-- title:
--   Theorem 2.20 — $\Pi:\mathcal W_\kappa(\Omega)\to\widehat{\mathcal W}_\kappa$ is continuous, and a quotient map for $\Omega=[0,1]$
-- statement:
--   Fix a probability space $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mu)$ and $\kappa\ge1$, and give the space $\mathcal W_\kappa(\Omega)=\mathcal W\times\Lambda_\kappa$ of $\kappa$-coloured graphons on $\Omega$ the product of the cut-norm topology on $\mathcal W$ and the weak-* topology on $\Lambda_\kappa$. Give $\widehat{\mathcal W}_\kappa$ its topology as a subspace of $\mathbb R^L$. Then the map
--   $$
--   \Pi:\mathcal W_\kappa(\Omega)\to\widehat{\mathcal W}_\kappa,\qquad \Pi(W,\Psi)=\tau(W,\Psi),
--   $$
--   is continuous. Moreover, for $\Omega=[0,1]$ with Lebesgue measure, $\Pi$ maps onto $\widehat{\mathcal W}_\kappa$ and is a quotient map, so $\widehat{\mathcal W}_\kappa$ is a topological quotient of $\mathcal W_\kappa([0,1])$.
--
--   Hence a map $f$ on coloured graph limits is continuous if and only if $f\circ\Pi$ is continuous on coloured graphons on $[0,1]$; this is how continuity of graph parameters is checked in §§4–5.
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\mathcal W_\kappa(\Omega)$ is `KGraphon μ κ` with the topology of §2.4. Continuity of $\Pi$ is stated into $\mathbb R^L$, which is continuity into the subspace $\widehat{\mathcal W}_\kappa$. The quotient clause is stated as: the range of $\Pi$ is $\widehat{\mathcal W}_\kappa$, and $\Pi$ corestricted to its range is a quotient map (`Topology.IsQuotientMap`). The page's "in other words" sentence is the universal property of quotient maps.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: W × Λκ with cut-norm topology × weak-* topology → KGraphon μ κ with the infimum of the topology generated by cut-norm balls and the coarsest topology making Ψ ↦ ∫ ψ_ℓ g dμ continuous (g integrable); points are functions, not a.e.-classes; reason: the balls form a basis of the pseudometric topology, the infimum of the pulled-back topologies is the product topology, and both topologies see only a.e.-classes
--   - encoding: Π is a quotient mapping onto Ŵ → range of Π = GraphLimitSpace κ, and Set.rangeFactorization Π is Topology.IsQuotientMap; reason: a quotient mapping is a surjection whose target carries the coinduced topology
--   - encoding: test graphs F ∈ U (unlabelled coloured graphs); limits as points of [0,1]^U → labelled coloured graphs on Fin (n+1) (LIdx κ); points of ℝ^L with the product topology; reason: t(F, ·) is invariant under relabelling (p.8), so equalities, convergence and limits over L and U agree; ℝ^L only changes the ambient space
--   - encoding: colourings Ψ : Ω → Δκ, colours [κ]; pure colourings χ : Ω → [κ] → Ψ : Ω → Fin κ → ℝ with measurable nonnegative coordinates summing to 1 (IsKColouring); pure χ measurable with the point-mass colouring ofColour χ; reason: the same objects (Definition 2.1, p.7), with colours numbered 0, …, κ − 1
--   - encoding: κ ≥ 1 (Definition 2.1) → [NeZero κ]; reason: the same condition
--   - encoding: [0, 1] with Lebesgue measure λ → unitInterval with volume and the Borel σ-field; reason: Remark 3.3: the Borel and Lebesgue σ-fields give the same results up to a.e. equality
-- source:
--   Janson, Lo, McDiarmid, Skeja, Skerman, Graphon Colourings and Limits of Graph Partitions, arXiv:2610.38599 (held version, sha256 3d198813a65d248922299a3380e10f238dbc5dcd3be060f7810b462bd5d3d10f), Theorem 2.20, p.12 (PDF p.12)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_GraphonCheeger_Supplement_KGraphon

open MeasureTheory Topology

universe u

namespace GraphonCheeger.Supplement

/-- Theorem 2.20, p.12 (Janson, Lo, McDiarmid, Skeja, Skerman, *Graphon Colourings and Limits of Graph Partitions*,
arXiv:2610.38599 (held version, sha256 3d198813…)): fix a probability space `(Ω, F, µ)` and give the space
`W_κ(Ω) = W × Λ_κ` of all `κ`-coloured graphons on it the product of the cut-norm topology on `W`
and the weak-* topology on `Λ_κ`; give `Ŵ_κ` its topology (§2.3.2). Then the map `Π` sending a
coloured graphon to its coloured graph limit is continuous. Moreover, for `(Ω, F, µ) = [0, 1]`,
`Π` is a quotient mapping; hence `Ŵ_κ` is a quotient space of `W_κ([0, 1])`.

**Formalization Note.**
* `W_κ(Ω)` is `KGraphon µ κ` with its topology (cut norm `×` weak-*, §2.4). `Π(W, Ψ) = τ(W, Ψ)`,
  valued in `ℝ^L` with the product topology; `Ŵ_κ = GraphLimitSpace κ` carries the subspace
  topology (Def. 3.5 (W1), Lemma 3.6), so continuity into `ℝ^L` is continuity into `Ŵ_κ`.
* "Quotient mapping" onto `Ŵ_κ`: the range of `Π` is `Ŵ_κ`, and `Π` corestricted to its range
  (`Set.rangeFactorization`) is a quotient map (`Topology.IsQuotientMap`); the paper's "in other
  words" sentence is the universal property of quotient maps and is not a separate claim.
* `κ ≥ 1` is `[NeZero κ]`. -/
theorem theorem_2_20 (κ : ℕ) [NeZero κ] {Ω : Type u} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] :
    Continuous (fun p : KGraphon μ κ => tau μ p.W p.Ψ) ∧
    Set.range (fun p : KGraphon (volume : Measure unitInterval) κ => tau volume p.W p.Ψ) =
      GraphLimitSpace κ ∧
    Topology.IsQuotientMap (Set.rangeFactorization
      (fun p : KGraphon (volume : Measure unitInterval) κ => tau volume p.W p.Ψ)) := by sorry

end GraphonCheeger.Supplement
